tdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;--gjdl5QipMBTdDz3h7L5RmVTy17Jm3zucJuwTlzU98ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2yUkwCFSg2LYoc-Xp3gqDj83pnzMfLvjEb2_nAAcG58ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkdTextƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;qWFtKKlKxQiX64mGvfzR0SI7OfIdMRx5064pYrZOQ9Aƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkqSyndicationSourceƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;VemKE1wkAfkHV0tS_HBEYocDA8F5sNNz9cp0JYAbNQIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$urn:rmn:predicate:source-reports-urlƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;qcyc5sysGpFLyNRWhy7hOnZQO0YHqjlWeNASNMg4decƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarktUnsaturatedPredicateƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;y6JLvmiGS-8eRPwJ1d920MGlLRZZcbaA_KK8mGsNHaIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarksGroundedPredicationƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2XgYJKe3Lk3Oj3jkWjG20y67aHtqq20NmbonH3llT4sƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Od7P66c7IBO2dpXRj8_5PwJmo1YFRRAKvKcncpp-S54ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;fD-V1WK3OL8ojIOQgH2UeS9_Av3kbSvNEVEDIAdDv6kƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx2026-07-23T22:08:03.088Zƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QUv8RnLbkpnL4FmGCTk7-XqVCaS3ccbPi8lcBqGej5sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxOhttps://grist.org/transportation/the-secret-to-a-better-grid-electric-vehicles/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;K0F5XK64Kp2nEZk-LT5R8I2EAI7Xr3m2f4O_7hkpCuwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9https://newscable-source-broker.invalid/activitypub/actorƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;OVZLdIy88oWQa5wm_WRmVQWVxKiBTWwq1w8cCHbE3Jwƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2PzZg8B2moG2W6D9vnMYWMYDT8JAl9HETBfbkr2DzLkƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxOhttps://grist.org/transportation/the-secret-to-a-better-grid-electric-vehicles/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;_rfZZG3Si-JQpKs_Z6Ec03xBdOT-9lZq_MJqkH_EOSsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyYou canâ€™t tell when youâ€™re passing one on the highway, but an electric vehicle may well have a superpower. It charges by plugging into the electric grid, of course, but it might also be able to send energy back into it. For a growing number of EVs, this vehicle-to-grid technology, or V2G, creates a revenue stream for drivers, who utilities pay to tap into their batteries when demand on the grid spikes.&nbsp; Utilities across the country are pursuing pilot projects for the tech, figuring out how to coordinate hundreds, thousands, and eventually millions of EVs. To that end, today a coalition of companies â€” Eversource, National Grid, EnergyHub, Sunrun, and the Mobility House â€” is launching an early test of the technology in Massachusetts so utility customers can give it a try. Lessons learned here and elsewhere could drive the technique into the mainstream, making the grid more reliable, and electricity cheaper , even if you donâ€™t own an EV. â€œItâ€™s great to have Massachusetts stepping into the lead here and doing this because these learnings are whatâ€™s going to allow this technology to scale,â€ said Chip Silverman, director of grid services at Sunrun, a solar and battery company. In the new system, participants hook into an existing program called ConnectedSolutions , which allows homes to run off their residential batteries, among other things. Then when thereâ€™s a â€œdemand responseâ€ event, like during a heat wave, EV batteries can help the grid keep up. Participants are compensated for this, just like some homeowners who provide energy from their solar panels.&nbsp; â€œItâ€™s really kind of a small number of hours per year that youâ€™re discharging the battery,â€ said Russell Vare, vice president of vehicle-grid integration at the Mobility House North America, a provider of charging infrastructure. â€œItâ€™s not necessarily like a daily discharge. Itâ€™s just during those peak times the events are called.â€ Utilities like V2G because theyâ€™re dealing with escalating, overlapping challenges. For one, the demand for electricity is growing as more power-hungry data centers come online and people switch from internal combustion cars to EVs and from gas furnaces to heat pumps . At the same time, utilities are trying to ditch gas and coal in favor of renewables like wind and solar. These clean technologies, though, are intermittent, in that the wind isnâ€™t always blowing and the sun isnâ€™t always shining. Utilities need ways to bank energy for later use, which is why theyâ€™re building storage facilities and even using the Earth itself as a giant battery . Already, Americans are struggling with skyrocketing energy prices for a variety of reasons. Utility shareholders are reaping profits, for instance, and customers are footing the bill for improvements, like burying power lines so they donâ€™t spark wildfires or come down in a storm . Building battery facilities and stringing transmission lines will add still more costs â€” the inevitable price to pay for a cleaner, more reliable grid. Read Next The US may be hitting a tipping point for heat pumps Matt Simon Except some of that spending may indeed be avoidable: The promise of V2G is that it can make electricity cheaper . While participants need a bidirectional charger to send energy back to the grid, a utility doesnâ€™t need to reinvent the system as a whole. EV batteries are an existing, widespread, and reliable source of backup energy to tap into in times of high demand, like during the summer when everyone is running their air conditioning. â€œItâ€™s the cheapest cost of flexible energy storage that will be available for the grid,â€ Vare said. The hotter the planet gets, the more enticing V2G looks for utilities. People need additional AC to stay healthy, but the appliances require lots of power to run. If that energy isnâ€™t coming from renewables, itâ€™s causing even more warming and increasing demand for AC, and on and on. In a V2G system, vehicles that follow predictable schedules, like school buses and municipal fleets, will be especially useful , in addition to their batteries being enormous stores of energy. Even a typical EV battery, though, has around six times the capacity as a backup unit mounted outside a house. While not all EVs have the hardware needed to tap into V2G, more models are, like the Nissan Leaf , coming equipped to do so. And as with any technology, bidirectional chargers will get cheaper over time. â€œAs hardware costs come down, installation becomes simpler, and standards continue to mature, we expect vehicle-to-grid to become dramatically more accessible over the next several years,â€ said Seth Frader-Thompson, president of EnergyHub, in a statement. With EVs sitting idle all across a city, that adds up to some serious power. So much so, in fact, that only a fraction of owners would need to participate for a program to adequately support the grid. But the more people who opt in, the better, as that gives a utility a broader base to draw from. â€œIf you have a higher number of batteries out there in a virtual power plant, you can actually use less energy from each individual battery,â€ Silverman said. â€œBut collectively, when you patch them all together and aggregate them, it comes out to a very large resource. And thatâ€™s really where the magic is.â€ These early projects are figuring out how best to coordinate this, like with apps that let people specify when they typically use their vehicle to, say, get to work. (Participants in the new Massachusetts program are able to do this.) The overall idea is for EVs to send power back to the grid when demand is highest, like when people return home from work and switch on appliances. Then, as the night goes on, the vehicles can charge. This would be paired with another technique, known as active managed charging, which staggers when EVs juice up through the evening and early morning hours â€” that is, theyâ€™re not all doing so at 10 p.m., which would suddenly and dramatically increase demand on the grid. All told, V2G promises to turn consumers of electricity into more active participants in the grid, with power flowing both ways. Far from straining the grid to the point of collapse, EVs will instead help save it.&nbsp; This story was originally published by Grist with the headline The secret to a better grid? Electric vehicles. on Jul 23, 2026.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;UjtQc5T1AdA4eu5ff8gSHIljNnemjhLVOJhuc--7CV0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;lwDBc8zGHDoDHdoOnm5Jrzej43pfgpeC8wKl8YNKv9cƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IqO4TybkQFLza7a38b5Hn0laNsRDNfADgrcYejZSbYgƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefE